# Heroville Migration Rules

Follow MODERNIZATION.md for the migration plan. These rules apply to all work on this codebase during the migration.

## Game Logic

- **Do not change game logic** (rules, formulas, win/loss conditions, economy, combat, progression) unless the user explicitly asks for a behavior change or bug fix.
- Game logic lives in `src/services/`. React components and hooks should **call** these services and **display** results; they should not reimplement or alter domain behavior.
- When fixing a bug in a service, add or extend unit tests so the fix is regression-tested.

## Testing After Work

- **After any change**, run **E2E** for regression and fix failures before considering the work done. Use `npm test` (build + Playwright). Unit tests are re-added during migration.
- When migrating a tab or feature: run E2E to verify current behavior first, then implement the React piece, then rerun E2E to confirm behavior is unchanged.

## Readability and Comments

- Prefer **readable code**: clear names, small functions, obvious control flow. Future you (or a human reviewer) should be able to follow the code without guessing.
- Add **comments** where they help a human reviewer:
  - **Why** something is done (non-obvious intent, workarounds, migration notes).
  - **What** a block or function is responsible for when the name isn’t enough.
- Avoid noise: don’t comment every line; do comment non-obvious “why” and non-obvious “what.”

## One-Off Tutorial Comments (Optional)

- For **new or heavily touched files**, you may add **one-off “tutorial” comments** meant for the human to read once and then delete. These help when the author returns to manually review the codebase.
- **Convention:** Use a clear tag so they’re easy to find and remove, e.g.:
  - `// [TUTORIAL] ...` — one-line explanation for first-time readers; safe to delete after reading.
  - Or a short block at the top of the file: `/* [TUTORIAL] This file does X. Key idea: Y. You can delete this block after reading. */`
- **Where to put them:** At the top of a new file (what this file is and how it fits in), or above a non-obvious section (e.g. “This hook exists because …”). Keep each tutorial comment short (1–3 sentences).
- The author may delete all `[TUTORIAL]` comments after they’ve read them; the rest of the comments should remain as ongoing documentation.

**Example:** At the top of a new React component:  
`// [TUTORIAL] This component renders the Town tab; it calls BuildingService and GameStateService. Delete this line after you've read it.`
